# BINO — Building Intelligence & Operations (product differentiation)

These principles govern **every** product, design, architecture, and engineering decision. Do **not** add generic features that do not strengthen this differentiation.

## What BINO is (and is not)

BINO’s differentiation is **not** the ability to open and manage tickets — many systems already do that.

BINO must be a system that builds **smart operational memory per building**: learn from ticket history, equipment, vendors, costs, and time-to-resolution; auto-recommend the right worker or vendor; detect recurring failures; alert early on SLA risk; and predict which systems may fail next.

Every new feature must advance the shift from a system that **records work** to one that **makes decisions, prevents failures, and proves to the management company how much time and money were saved**.

## North-star product metrics

Prioritize and surface these metrics (product UI, APIs, analytics, demos):

| Metric (HE) | Intent |
|-------------|--------|
| זמן עד שיוך | Time to assignment |
| זמן עד פתרון | Time to resolution |
| שיעור תקלות חוזרות | Recurring-failure rate |
| עלות תחזוקה לבניין | Maintenance cost per building |
| אחוז התקלות שטופלו ללא התערבות מנהל | % tickets handled without manager intervention |

## Decision gate (required before building)

Reject or reshape work that fails this gate:

1. Does it deepen **operational memory** (history → learning → reusable judgment)?
2. Does it **automate a decision** (assign, escalate, recommend vendor/worker) or **prevent** a failure?
3. Does it **prove savings** (time/money) to the management company?
4. Does it move a north-star metric above?

If the answer is “only better ticket CRUD / generic SaaS” → do not ship it as a priority feature unless the user explicitly overrides.

